React Native 0.88: hover tracking holds the nodes it dispatches pointer events to.

PointerHoverTracker::getLatestNode returned the newest clone of a node as a raw
pointer taken from the shared_ptr getNewestCloneOfShadowNode returns, owned only
by the shadow tree's current revision. handleIncomingPointerEventOnNode
collects the nodes a pointer leaves that way, then dispatches pointerleave to
them one by one from the deepest. Each dispatch runs JavaScript, and meanwhile
the UI thread may commit a state update and mount it (ShadowTree::commit, then
MountingCoordinator::pullTransaction), which frees the revision those nodes
belong to: the next dispatch reads a freed node. On OxyOS that crashed the
host on its JavaScript thread in the dispatch's ShadowNode::getEventEmitter,
with MALLOC_PERTURB_'s 0xa5 in the register (a Dropsea window under load).

EventPath now owns its nodes: the target through target_, each ancestor through
root_ (whose tree it is part of), a newest clone through the shared_ptr that
found it. The events, their order and their targets are unchanged.

PointerEventsProcessor.cpp's own header is included by its full path, as the
patched copy is compiled outside React Native's directory.

Upstream (facebook/react-native main) still returns the raw pointer at the
time of writing. CMakeLists.txt applies this to private copies of the three
files at configure time; the React Native package itself is never modified,
and a release this no longer applies to stops the build.

